<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<subsection xml:id="course_description" label="course_description" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Course Description</title>
  <p>
    This course is a continuation of MATH 4083, and it will utilize the rings and groups developed there to study fields and their extensions.
  </p>
  <p>
    We first return to rings, where we study prime and maximal ideals, principal ideal domains, and polynomial rings with coefficients in a field, since quotients of these polynomial rings are the means by which we construct new fields from old ones.
    We then study field extensions in detail, including algebraic extensions, splitting fields, and normal and separable extensions.
  </p>
  <p>
    The course culminates in the Fundamental Theorem of Galois Theory, which states that the intermediate fields of a Galois extension correspond to the subgroups of its Galois group, so that questions about fields and polynomials may be translated into questions about finite groups.
    As in MATH 4083, an equally important goal of this course is to improve students' ability to read and write mathematical proofs.
  </p>
</subsection>
